  <content>
    <section class="practice">
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
        <exercise id="db3e5236-c2a2-49c7-804d-f43ce4d36a8b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
            <para>The quadratic equation \(x^2+7x+10=0\) is in the form of \(ax^2+bx+c=0\).</para>
            <para>What is the value of \(a\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1. The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="acb22ad4-e36a-4c14-b252-8214e407a7d2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the value of \(b\).</para>
            <para>What is the value of \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The value of \(b\) is 7.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 7. The value of \(b\) is 7.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_78372383-13cd-404a-8b99-0cb68702154a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
            <para>What is the value of \(c\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The value of \(c\) is 10.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 10. The value of \(c\) is 10.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0d72bfb3-3bc6-42ee-8980-b3f3663afc70" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Examine the solution method that has been started below and use it to answer parts d and e.</para>
            <para>Original equation \(x^2+7x+10=0\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Subtract 10 from each side. \(x^2+7x=-10\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply each side by 4. \(4x^2+4(7x)=-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +\_\_\_\_^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \((2x+\_\_\_\_)^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \(2x+\_\_\_\_= \pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \(2x=\_\_\_\_\pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\) \(x= \_\_\_\_\)</para>
            <para>In Step 2, what might be a good reason for multiplying each side of the equation by 4?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>You must always multiply both sides by 4 when completing the square.</para>
            <para>If the value of \(c\) is positive and you multiply by 4, it will be easier to divide the \(b\) term by 3 before you square it.</para>
            <para>Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the \(b\) term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
            <para>Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a625e32e-f588-4181-a0ab-9075ea8737d1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Complete the unfinished steps, then <emphasis>select two</emphasis> solutions.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The solutions are -5 and -2.</para>
            <para>Here are the unfinished steps:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial. - Factor the trinomial. \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. - Take the square root of each side. \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides. - Subtract 7 from both sides. \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Solve. - Solve. \(x=-5\), -2 \(x=-5\), -2</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are: -5 and -2.</para>
            <para>Here are the unfinished steps:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\). \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial. - Factor the trinomial. \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\) \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. - Take the square root of each side. \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides. - Subtract 7 from both sides. \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\) \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Solve. - Solve. \(x=-5\), -2 \(x=-5\), -2</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1f83ae39-a00f-4bd9-8b72-550d1843c7a6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Substitute the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula, \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\), but do not evaluate any of the expressions. Explain how this expression is related to solving \(x^2+7x+10=0\) by completing the square.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>In both forms of solving the equation, you need to list the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) to determine the solutions.</para>
            <para>Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
            <para>They are related because neither one can have a negative \(c\) value.</para>
            <para>For this equation, the quadratic formula and completing the square are not the same and therefore not related.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f30fb96e-9c43-4d25-8acb-2c705204e8eb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
            <para>Consider the standard form of the equation \(x^2−39=0\).</para>
            <para>What is the value of \(a\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1. The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9310921f-1e37-4cb5-894d-00b995a9df75" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the value of \(b\).</para>
            <para>What is the value of \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The value of \(b\) is 0.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0. The value of \(b\) is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_44da0935-583a-49f8-8fbf-42395ea81a40" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
            <para>What is the value of \(c\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The value of \(c\) is -39.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -39. The value of \(c\) is -39.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_69305262-192e-44aa-bd03-412bc5c66070" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Can you use the quadratic formula to solve this equation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The quadratic formula works for all quadratic equations. Substituting the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula and evaluating the expression gives the solutions.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 – 9.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is yes. The quadratic formula works for all quadratic equations. Substituting the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula and evaluating the expression gives the solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_61fb1551-01ea-42b2-ac7d-a9bb6e748245" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Can you solve this equation using square roots?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Yes, the solutions are \(\pm 39\).</para>
            <para>No, there is no solution.</para>
            <para>Yes, the solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
            <para>No, this equation can only be solved by the quadratic formula.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes, adding 39 to both sides of the equation gives \(x^2=39\). The solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Yes, adding 39 to both sides of the equation gives \(x^2=39\). The solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_8f682d62-d494-473f-8d61-49e01216ec73" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>She arrived at this equation: \((2ax+b)^2=b^2−4ac\).</para>
            <para>Clare is deriving the quadratic formula by solving \(ax^2+bx+c=0\) by completing the square.</para>
            <para>Choose the best description of what she needs to do to finish solving for \(x\).</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Subtract \(b^2\) from each side. Find the square roots of each side. Then divide each side by 2.</para>
            <para>Find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para>
            <para>Divide each side by 2. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then find the square roots of each side.</para>
            <para>Find the square roots of each side. Divide each side by 2. Subtract \(b \) from each side.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! She needs to find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 – 9.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: She needs to find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
